# Comparison: `===` vs `==` ⚖️

This is one of the most critical concepts in JavaScript!

- **Strict Equality (`===`)**: Compares **both value AND type**. No implicit type coercion occurs. (Always use this!)
- **Loose Equality (`==`)**: Coerces operands to the same type before comparing. Leads to subtle bugs!

```javascript
console.log(5 === "5"); // false (number !== string)
console.log(5 == "5");  // true  (string "5" coerced to number 5!)
console.log(null === undefined); // false
console.log(null == undefined);  // true
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
console.log("Strict comparison 5 === '5':", 5 === "5");
console.log("Loose comparison 5 == '5':", 5 == "5");
console.log("Strict 0 === false:", 0 === false);
console.log("Loose 0 == false:", 0 == false);
console.log("Strict null === undefined:", null === undefined);
console.log("Loose null == undefined:", null == undefined);
FULL_CODE_END -->

# Logical Operators (AND, OR, NOT) 🧠

Combine boolean conditions:

```javascript
let isAdult = true;
let hasTicket = false;

// 1. Logical AND (&&): true only if both operands are true
console.log(isAdult && hasTicket); // false

// 2. Logical OR (||): true if at least one operand is true
console.log(isAdult || hasTicket); // true

// 3. Logical NOT (!): inverts the boolean value
console.log(!isAdult); // false
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
