# NaN: Not-a-Number ⚠️

`NaN` represents a computational error when a mathematical operation cannot produce a meaningful number:

```javascript
console.log("apple" * 5); // NaN
console.log(typeof NaN);  // "number" (Another famous JS quirk!)

// NaN is the ONLY value in JS that does not equal itself!
console.log(NaN === NaN); // false!

// Check for NaN with Number.isNaN:
console.log(Number.isNaN("apple" * 5)); // true
```

